Lab 6: Managing Infrastructure Security in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Secure OpenStack component files with proper permissions
Implement TLS encryption for service communication
Audit OpenStack services for security compliance
Prerequisites
Running OpenStack deployment (minimum: Keystone, Nova, Glance)
sudo/root access to controller and compute nodes
Basic Linux command line proficiency
OpenSSL package installed
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient)
Task 1: Securing Service Component Files
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current File Permissions
Check current permissions on critical OpenStack configuration files:
ls -l /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
ls -l /etc/nova/nova.conf
ls -l /etc/glance/glance-api.conf
Expected Output:
-rw-r----- 1 root keystone 1234 Jun 1 10:00 /etc/keystone/keystone.conf

Subtask 1.2: Set Proper Permissions
Set restrictive permissions (owner: root, group: service, mode: 640):
sudo chown root:keystone /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
sudo chmod 640 /etc/keystone/keystone.conf

sudo chown root:nova /etc/nova/nova.conf
sudo chmod 640 /etc/nova/nova.conf
Verify changes:
stat -c "%a %U %G" /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Expected Output:
640 root keystone

Troubleshooting:
If services fail after permission changes, verify service logs (journalctl -u keystone)

Task 2: Implementing TLS Encryption
Subtask 2.1: Generate SSL Certificates
Create Certificate Authority (CA):
openssl genrsa -out ca.key 4096
openssl req -new -x509 -days 365 -key ca.key -out ca.crt
Generate server certificate:
openssl genrsa -out server.key 2048
openssl req -new -key server.key -out server.csr
openssl x509 -req -in server.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out server.crt -days 365
Subtask 2.2: Configure Keystone for HTTPS
Edit /etc/keystone/keystone.conf:
[ssl]
enable = True
certfile = /etc/keystone/ssl/certs/server.crt
keyfile = /etc/keystone/ssl/private/server.key
ca_certs = /etc/keystone/ssl/certs/ca.crt
Restart Keystone:
sudo systemctl restart apache2
Verification:

curl -v https://<controller-ip>:5000
Expected Output:
Should show TLS handshake success without certificate warnings

Task 3: Security Auditing
Subtask 3.1: Install OpenStack Auditing Tools
Install openstack-ansible-security:
sudo apt install git
git clone https://opendev.org/openstack/openstack-ansible-security
cd openstack-ansible-security
Subtask 3.2: Run Security Audit
Execute the audit playbook:
ansible-playbook playbooks/security-hardening.yml
Review findings in /var/log/openstack_audit/
Key Findings to Check:

Unencrypted communication channels
Overly permissive file permissions
Default credentials in use
Subtask 3.3: Remediate Findings
Example remediation for password policy:
openstack domain set --password-policy min_password_length=12 default
Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Secured OpenStack configuration files with proper ownership and permissions
Implemented TLS encryption for service communication
Performed security auditing and basic remediation
Next Steps:

